The '''McLaren P1''' (codenamed P12)<ref name=cad/> is a [[flagship]] [[sports car]] produced by British marque [[McLaren Automotive]]. Styled by American [[car designer]] [[Frank Stephenson]], it is the second installment in McLaren's Ultimate Series after the [[McLaren F1]]. Considered to be the [[spiritual successor]] to the F1, the P1 was one of the first high performance sports cars to be introduced incorporating [[Hybrid electric vehicle|hybrid technology]]; the [[Porsche 918 Spyder]] having begun taking orders prior to the P1 and the [[LaFerrari]] introduced alongside it. First shown as a concept on the 20th anniversary of the F1 at the [[2012 Paris Motor Show]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Undercoffler|first=David|title=McLaren's wild P1 supercar breaks cover|url=http://www.latimes.com/business/money/la-fi-mo-autos-mclaren-p1-debut-20120918,0,4742776.story|access-date=19 September 2012|newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=18 September 2012|archive-date=25 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131025220844/http://www.latimes.com/business/money/la-fi-mo-autos-mclaren-p1-debut-20120918,0,4742776.story|url-status=live}}</ref> the P1 made its debut at the 2013 [[Geneva International Motor Show]].<ref name= Deliveries>{{cite web | url = http://www.autoblog.com/2013/10/21/mclaren-p1-official-performance-figures/ | title = McLaren P1 hits 60 mph in 2.6 seconds, 186 in 16.5 | first = Noah | last = Joseph | publisher = [[Autoblog.com]] | date = 21 October 2013 | access-date = 21 October 2013 | archive-date = 21 October 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131021134958/http://www.autoblog.com/2013/10/21/mclaren-p1-official-performance-figures/ | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=Soldout2 />

In similar fashion to the F1, the P1 is [[mid-engined]], [[rear wheel drive]], and has a [[carbon fibre]] [[monocoque]]. Stephenson drew inspiration for parts of the car from a [[sailfish]] he saw when on holiday in Miami.<ref>{{cite web |last=Chawla |first=Dalmeet |date=28 August 2014 |title=The car designer who turned a sailfish into a supercar |url=https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20140828-how-a-fish-inspired-a-supercar |access-date=5 July 2020 |website=[[BBC]] |archive-date=25 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200225132432/https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20140828-how-a-fish-inspired-a-supercar |url-status=live }}</ref> In all, 375 units were produced, with several special editions such as the non-road legal P1 GTR and P1 LM among others having smaller production runs. Several pre-production prototypes utilised by McLaren for testing were later refurbished, modified and sold to customers.<ref>{{cite web |title=A McLaren P1 Experimental Prototype is for sale |url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1113936_a-mclaren-p1-experimental-prototype-is-for-sale |website=Motor Authority |date=22 November 2017 |access-date=3 December 2018 |first=Jeff |last=Glucker |archive-date=3 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181203202727/https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1113936_a-mclaren-p1-experimental-prototype-is-for-sale |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/3037656849|id={{ProQuest|3037656849}}|access-date=9 June 2024|title=Japan race pure artwork from a Dutch master|date=13 April 2024|page=40|newspaper=[[Weekend Australian]]|archive-date=10 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093215/https://www.proquest.com/docview/3037656849|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:2013 McLaren P1 MSO Rear LC24.jpg|thumb|2013 McLaren P1 MSO rear view]]

== Background ==
Following the conclusion of [[McLaren F1]] production in 1998, [[McLaren Automotive]] (then McLaren Cars) became [[Dormant company|dormant]] until 2010.<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[Robb Report]]|url=https://robbreport.com/motors/cars/gallery/fascinating-facts-mclaren-1235611308/2104-mclaren12c/#!|title=8 Fascinating Facts You Never Knew About McLaren|first=Bob|last=Sorokanich<!--if anyone would like to question the reliability of the source, Sorokanich used to be the head of the editorial team for Road & Track-->|date=9 May 2024|access-date=10 June 2024|archive-date=10 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093323/https://pandg.tapad.com/tag?gdpr=0&referrer_url=&page_url=https%3A%2F%2Frobbreport.com%2Fmotors%2Fcars%2Fgallery%2Ffascinating-facts-mclaren-1235611308%2F2104-mclaren12c%2F&owner=P%26G&bp_id=penskemedia&ch=%7B%22architecture%22%3A%22%22%2C%22bitness%22%3A%22%22%2C%22brands%22%3A%5B%5D%2C%22mobile%22%3Afalse%2C%22model%22%3A%22%22%2C%22platformVersion%22%3A%22%22%7D&initiator=js&data=%7B%22category%22%3A%22Motors%22%7D#!|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[McLaren 12C]] was McLaren's first production car following the F1, introduced in 2011.<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[Road and Track]]|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/road-tests/a16925/2012-mclaren-mp4-12c-1/|title=2012 McLaren MP4-12C Supercar Road Test|first=Patrick|last=Hong|date=10 February 2011|access-date=10 June 2024|archive-date=29 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329224934/https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/road-tests/a16925/2012-mclaren-mp4-12c-1/|url-status=live}}</ref> With the 12C in production, McLaren announced in a press release in 2011 that a new factory (the [[McLaren Production Centre]]) had opened to ramp up production. Mentioned in the same press release were plans for several "game-changing high performance cars".<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[MotorTrend]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200811105425/https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-hints-at-game-changing-future-cars-as-new-production-center-opens-138337/|archive-date=11 August 2020|access-date=10 June 2024|url-status=dead|url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-hints-at-game-changing-future-cars-as-new-production-center-opens-138337/|date=21 November 2011|first=Jake|last=Holmes|title=McLaren Hints at 'Game Changing' Future Cars as New Production Center Opens}}</ref> British automotive magazine [[Autocar (magazine)|''Autocar'']] reported earlier in the year that a new flagship model, internally designated the "Mega Mac", was under development, with production set to be limited to 500 units.<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]|url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/mega-mac-eclipse-veyron|title='Mega Mac' to eclipse Veyron| date=14 May 2011|access-date=10 June 2024|archive-date=29 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240329030958/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/mega-mac-eclipse-veyron|url-status=live}}</ref> Other specifications included a {{cvt|5|L|cuin|1|abbr=on}} litre [[V8]] that was set to produce {{cvt|799|hp|PS kW|0|abbr=on}}, and performance intended to rival that of the [[Bugatti Veyron]].<ref name="mt">{{cite magazine|magazine=[[MotorTrend]]|url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-p1-supercar-shown-ahead-of-paris-show-debut-262753/|title=McLaren P1 Supercar Shown Ahead of Paris Show Debut|first=Jake|last=Holmes|access-date=10 June 2024|date=18 September 2012|archive-date=24 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240324143540/https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-p1-supercar-shown-ahead-of-paris-show-debut-262753/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The first teaser was shown to the public in September 2012. Alongside a vague visual depiction of the car, British automotive magazine [[Car (magazine)|''car'']] reported that American designer [[Frank Stephenson]] was to be in charge of the exterior design of the P1, along with powertrain details. The engine was expected to be an upgraded version of the [[M838T]] engine found in the [[McLaren MP4-12C]] mated to a [[KERS]] system.<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=[[Car (magazine)|car]]|url=https://www.carmagazine.co.uk/car-news/first-official-pictures/mclaren/mclaren-p12-supercar-2012-first-teaser-shot/|title=McLaren P12 supercar (2012) first teaser shot|first=Ollie|last=Kew|date=5 September 2012|access-date=7 June 2024|archive-date=18 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230118064706/https://www.carmagazine.co.uk/car-news/first-official-pictures/mclaren/mclaren-p12-supercar-2012-first-teaser-shot/|url-status=live}}</ref> The first actual photos of the P1 concept were revealed days later ahead of its public debut.<ref name=mt/>

A pre-production conceptual version of the P1 was shown at the [[2012 Paris Motor Show]], with further details of the performance of the P1. Aerodynamic performance was quoted at {{cvt|600|kg|lbs|0|abbr=on}} of [[downforce]] "well below maximum speed", a drag coefficient of {{cd|0.34}}, and the revelation of the active aero components within the P1.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.carmagazine.co.uk/car-news/first-official-pictures/mclaren/mclaren-p1---full-story-at-paris-motor-show-2012/|title=McLaren P1 – full story at Paris motor show 2012|date=27 September 2012|access-date=7 June 2024|first=Tim|last=Pollard|magazine=[[Car (magazine)|car]]|archive-date=7 June 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240607094907/https://www.carmagazine.co.uk/car-news/first-official-pictures/mclaren/mclaren-p1---full-story-at-paris-motor-show-2012/|url-status=live}}</ref> Details about the P1's powertrain were scant; American automotive magazine ''[[MotorTrend]]'' reported that according to McLaren insiders, the P1 was to have a [[plug-in hybrid]] powertrain. This was in line with earlier reports by British magazine ''[[Car (magazine)|car]]''. The P1's performance relative to the Veyron was also clarified, with McLaren having "no interest in out-Veyroning the Veyron", instead the P1's performance was to be more track-oriented.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-p1-concept-first-look/|title=First Look: McLaren P1 Concept|date=27 September 2012|first=Martyn|last=Goddard|access-date=10 June 2024|magazine=[[MotorTrend]]|archive-date=24 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240324151942/https://www.motortrend.com/news/mclaren-p1-concept-first-look/|url-status=live}}</ref> McLaren Automotive Managing Director [[Antony Sheriff]] echoed the sentiment at the P1's Paris debut, saying that the P1's aim "is not necessarily to be the fastest in absolute top speed but to be the quickest and most rewarding series production road car on a circuit".<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=Classic Driver|url=https://www.classicdriver.com/en/article/best-driver%E2%80%99s-car-world-mclaren-announces-new-p1|title=Best Driver's Car in the World? McLaren announces the new P1|first=Charis|last=Whitcombe|date=18 September 2012|access-date=10 June 2024|archive-date=8 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211208054307/https://www.classicdriver.com/en/article/best-driver%E2%80%99s-car-world-mclaren-announces-new-p1|url-status=live}}</ref> The production version of the P1 was unveiled at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2013|2013 Geneva Motor Show]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.motoring.com.au/news/2013/sports/mclaren/geneva-motor-show-mclaren-p1-unveiled-35288|title=Geneva Motor Show: McLaren P1 unveiled|publisher=Motoring|date=27 February 2013|access-date=9 September 2013|archive-date=16 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130616235336/http://www.motoring.com.au/news/2013/sports/mclaren/geneva-motor-show-mclaren-p1-unveiled-35288|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Specifications==
The P1 features a {{cvt|3799|cc|L cuin|1|abbr=on|order=flip}} [[twin-turbocharged]] [[V8 engine]].<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kb8tGX-HPQE&t=2m3s] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160618104901/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kb8tGX-HPQE&t=2m3s|date=18 June 2016}} McLaren's Chris Goodwin explains the P1 Drivetrain</ref> The twin turbos boost the [[petrol engine]] at {{cvt|1.4|bar|psi|1}} to deliver {{cvt|737|PS|kW PS hp|0|order=out}} at 7,500 rpm and {{convert|531|lbft|Nm|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 4,000 rpm, combined with an in-house-developed [[electric motor]] producing {{cvt|179|PS|kW PS hp|0|order=out}} and {{convert|192|lbft|Nm|0|abbr=on}} of torque.
The motor is the McLaren [[McLaren M838T engine|M838TQ]]
[[File:McLaren M838T (in MP4-12C chassis).jpg | thumb| The basis of the M838TQ, the M838T in the MP4-12C, the predecessor of P1.]]
The electric motor and the petrol engine in the P1, produce a combined power output of {{cvt|916|PS|kW PS hp|0|order=out}} and {{convert|900|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque.<ref>{{Cite web|title=McLaren P1- Specification|publisher=McLaren Automotive|url=https://cars.mclaren.com/gb-en/legacy/mclaren-p1/specification|access-date=2020-11-17|website=cars.mclaren.com|archive-date=12 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210112062608/https://cars.mclaren.com/gb-en/legacy/mclaren-p1/specification|url-status=live}}</ref> The electric motor can be deployed manually by the driver or left in automatic mode, whereby the car's [[Electronic control unit|ECU]]s 'torque fill' the gaps in the petrol engine's output, which is considered turbo lag. This gives the powertrain an effective powerband of almost 7,000&nbsp;rpm.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kb8tGX-HPQE&t=22m11s|title=Chris Harris Explains Torque Fill|work=YouTube|access-date=5 October 2014|archive-date=1 July 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140701194633/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Kb8tGX-HPQE&t=22m11s|url-status=live}}</ref> The car has [[rear mid-engine, rear-wheel-drive]] layout and is equipped with a 7-speed [[dual-clutch transmission]] developed by [[Graziano Trasmissioni]].<ref name=cad/>

Power for the electric motor is stored in a 324-cell [[lithium-ion]] high-density [[battery pack]] located behind the cabin, developed by [[Johnson Matthey Battery Systems]]. The battery can be charged by the engine or through a plug-in equipment and can be fully charged in two hours. The car can be operated using either the petrol engine, the electric motor or with a combination of the two. The P1 has an [[all-electric range]] of at least {{convert|10|km|abbr=on}} on the combined European drive cycle.<ref name="TopSpeed2">{{cite news |title=2014 McLaren P1 |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2014-mclaren-p1-ar153561/|first=Tom | last = Burkart|access-date=28 October 2013 |publisher=TopSpeed |date=21 October 2013}}</ref> Under the [[EPA]] cycle, the range in EV mode is {{convert|19|mi|abbr=on}}. During EV mode the P1 has a petrol consumption of 4.8g/100&nbsp;mile, and as a result, EPA's all-electric range is rated as zero. The total range is {{Convert|330|mi|abbr=on|0}}.<ref name=EPAMcLP1>{{cite web|url=http://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/Find.do?action=sbs&id=35600|title=Compare Side-by-Side - 2014 McLaren Automotive P1|work=[[U. S. Environmental Protection Agency]] and [[U.S. Department of Energy]]|date=12 September 2014|access-date=12 September 2014|archive-date=13 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140913033106/http://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/Find.do?action=sbs&id=35600|url-status=live}}</ref> The P1 combined fuel economy in EV mode was rated by the EPA at 18 [[miles per gallon gasoline equivalent|MPGe]] (13 L petrol equivalent/100&nbsp;km; 22 mpg-imp petrol equivalent), with an energy consumption of 25&nbsp;kW-hrs/100&nbsp;mi and petrol consumption of 4.8 gal<sub>-US</sub>/100&nbsp;mi. The combined fuel economy when running only with petrol is {{Convert|17|mpgUS|abbr=on}}, {{Convert|16|mpgUS|abbr=on}} for city driving, and {{Convert|20|mpgUS|abbr=on}} in highway.<ref name=EPAMcLP1/><ref name=EPA2014>{{Fuel Economy Guide |year=2014 |page=35}}</ref>

The P1 has Formula 1 derived features such as the Instant Power Assist System (IPAS), which gives an instant boost in acceleration via the electric motor, a [[Drag reduction system|Drag Reduction System (DRS)]] which operates the car's rear wing, thereby increasing straight line speed, and a [[Kinetic energy recovery system|KERS]]. Both of these features (IPAS, DRS) are operated via two buttons on the steering wheel. It also generates a downforce of 600&nbsp;kg at {{cvt|160|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.topgear.com/car-news/geneva-motor-show/meet-production-mclaren-p1 |title=Meet the production McLaren P1 |work=Top Gear |date=5 March 2013 |access-date=18 July 2016 |archive-date=18 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160318161100/http://www.topgear.com/car-news/geneva-motor-show/meet-production-mclaren-p1 |url-status=live }}</ref> and it boasts of a drag coefficient of only {{cd|0.34}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.topgear.com/car-news/paris-motor-show/officially-official-new-mclaren-p1 |title=Officially Official: the new McLaren P1 |work=Top Gear |date=27 September 2012 |access-date=18 July 2016 |archive-date=7 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160807021518/http://www.topgear.com/car-news/paris-motor-show/officially-official-new-mclaren-p1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

According to McLaren the P1 accelerates from {{cvt|0-100|km/h|mph|0}} in 2.8 seconds, {{cvt|0-200|km/h|mph|0}} in 6.8 seconds, and {{cvt|0-300|km/h|mph|0}} in 16.5 seconds, making it a full 5.5 seconds faster than the F1, and a standing quarter mile is claimed in 9.8 seconds at {{convert|152|mph|abbr=on|order=flip}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.autoblog.com/2013/10/21/mclaren-p1-official-performance-figures/|title=Autoblog Directory|work=Autoblog|date=21 October 2013 |access-date=5 October 2014|archive-date=21 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131021134958/http://www.autoblog.com/2013/10/21/mclaren-p1-official-performance-figures/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Autocar (magazine)|''Autocar'']] tested {{convert|0-60|mph|0|abbr=on}} mph in 2.8 seconds, {{convert|0-120|mph|0|abbr=on}} mph in 6.9 seconds, the standing quarter mile in 10.2 seconds at {{convert|147.5|mph|0|abbr=on}}, and the standing kilometre in 18.2 seconds at {{convert|178.5|mph|0|abbr=on}}.<ref>''Autocar'' road test No 5164</ref> In electric only mode 0-60&nbsp;mph in 10 seconds.<ref>{{Cite web|title=McLaren P1 Owner Reveals Battery Trickle Charger Costs $30,000|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/436104/mclaren-p1-owner-reveals-charger-cost/|access-date=2021-12-14|website=Motor1.com|language=en|archive-date=14 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214073419/https://www.motor1.com/news/436104/mclaren-p1-owner-reveals-charger-cost/|url-status=live}}</ref> The P1 is electronically limited to a top speed of {{cvt|350|km/h|mph|0}}. The P1 has a dry weight of {{convert|1395|kg|lb|abbr=on}}, giving it a [[power-to-weight ratio]] of 656&nbsp;PS/tonne. It has a kerb weight of {{cvt|1547|kg|lb|0}}<ref name="Autocar">{{cite news|title=McLaren P1 Review|url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/mclaren/p1|first=Steve | last = Sutcliffe|access-date=7 May 2014|publisher=Autocar|date=7 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140326103046/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/mclaren/p1|archive-date=26 March 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> which translates to 601&nbsp;PS/&nbsp;tonne. The P1 also features bespoke Pirelli P-Zero Corsa tyres and specially developed carbon-ceramic rotor, caliper and brake pads from [[Akebono Brake Industry|Akebono]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Akebono Reveals the Details of the Brake System Adopted by McLaren's P1 Ultrahigh Performance Road Car｜News｜Akebono Brake Industry Co., Ltd. |url=https://www.akebono-brake.com/static/news/2013/03/05/ne20130305_01.html |website=www.akebono-brake.com |access-date=2 February 2020 |archive-date=2 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200202200339/https://www.akebono-brake.com/static/news/2013/03/05/ne20130305_01.html |url-status=live }}</ref> According to McLaren it takes 6.2 seconds to brake from {{convert|300|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on}} to standstill, during which the car will cover 246 metres. From {{cvt|60|mph|km/h|0}}, it will cover 30.2 metres.<ref name="TopSpeed">{{cite news |title=2014 McLaren P1 |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2014-mclaren-p1-ar153561/ |access-date=10 October 2013 |publisher=TopSpeed |date=10 October 2013}}</ref>

==Production==
In August 2013 McLaren announced that the production allocation destined to the [[Americas]], [[Asia-Pacific]] and the [[Middle East]] was sold out.<ref name=Soldout>{{cite news|url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/mclaren-p1/mclaren-p1-virtually-sold-out|title=McLaren P1 "virtually sold out"|first=Mark | last = Tisshaw|work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]|date=30 August 2013|access-date=9 September 2013|archive-date=1 September 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130901214454/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/mclaren-p1/mclaren-p1-virtually-sold-out|url-status=live}}</ref> The cars destined for Europe were sold out by mid November 2013.<ref name=Soldout2>{{cite news|url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/mclaren-p1/mclaren-p1-sold-out|title=McLaren P1 sold out|first=Mark | last = Tisshaw|work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]|date=13 November 2013|access-date=23 November 2013|archive-date=27 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170427002828/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/mclaren-p1/mclaren-p1-sold-out|url-status=live}}</ref> The United States accounted for 34% of the limited production run, and Europe for 26%.<ref name="McLP12015">{{cite news |last=Moss |first=Darren |date=10 December 2015 |title=McLaren P1 production comes to an end |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/mclaren-p1-production-comes-end |access-date=25 January 2016 |work=Autocar UK |archive-date=16 August 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230816213152/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/mclaren-p1-production-comes-end |url-status=live }}</ref>

After some delays, production began in October 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://insideevs.com/mclaren-p1-officially-enters-production/|title=McLaren P1 Officially Enters Production|first=Eric | last = Loveday|publisher=InsideEVs.com|date=16 October 2013|access-date=16 October 2013|archive-date=17 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131017071240/http://insideevs.com/mclaren-p1-officially-enters-production/|url-status=live}}</ref> Hand-assembled by a team of 61 engineers, at a production rate of one car per day McLaren production was planned for fifty cars by the end of 2013.<ref name=Production>{{cite web|url=http://insideevs.com/mclaren-quickly-sells-two-thirds-of-total-production-run-of-p1-plug-in-hybrid/|title=McLaren Quickly Sells Two-Thirds of Total Production Run of P1 Plug-In Hybrid| first=Eric | last = Loveday|publisher=Inside EVs|date=6 May 2013|access-date=6 May 2013|archive-date=3 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130603131644/http://insideevs.com/mclaren-quickly-sells-two-thirds-of-total-production-run-of-p1-plug-in-hybrid/|url-status=live}}</ref> The first delivery to a retail customer took place at the company's headquarters in [[Woking]], England, in October 2013,<ref name=Deliveries/> with 12 units manufactured by mid November 2013.<ref name=Soldout2/> The first P1 delivery in the U.S. occurred in May 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://insideevs.com/jay-leno-gets-first-mclaren-p1-drives-heck-video/|title=Jay Leno Gets First McLaren P1, Drives The Heck Out Of It On Normal Roads – video| first=Jay | last = Cole|publisher=InsideEVs.com|date=13 May 2014|access-date=30 May 2014|archive-date=17 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140517201444/http://insideevs.com/jay-leno-gets-first-mclaren-p1-drives-heck-video/|url-status=live}}</ref> The production run ended in December 2015.<ref name=McLP12015/>

===Recalls===
In January 2016, the [[NHTSA]] issued a recall for 122 2014–2015 [[model year]] P1 cars manufactured between 1 March 2013 and 31 October 2015 due to a faulty secondary [[Bonnet (car)|bonnet]] latch. The concern was that if the primary latch were to snap, the secondary latch would be unable to catch the bonnet, leading to it opening whilst driving. All replacements were done at no cost to the owner.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/mclaren-recalls-a-large-number-of-p1s-in-the-us-103457.html|title=McLaren Recalls a Large Number of P1s in the U.S.|first=Alexandru|last=Monenciu|date=6 January 2016|access-date=11 June 2024|work=[[autoevolution]]}}</ref>

==Variants==
===P1 GTR (2015&ndash;2016)===
[[File:McLaren P1 GTR at Grand Basel 2018 03.jpg|left|thumb|P1 GTR exterior]]
[[File:2018 McLaren P1 GTR interior.jpg|thumb|P1 GTR interior|left]]Celebrating 20 years since their victory in the [[1995 24 Hours of Le Mans]], McLaren announced that they would resurrect the GTR name by launching a track-only version of the P1, the P1 GTR.

The P1 GTR was initially only available to P1 owners. The [[concept car]] made its debut at the 2014 [[Pebble Beach Concours d'Elegance]] in August 2014. The P1 GTR production model was officially unveiled at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2015|2015 Geneva Motor Show]].<ref name=P1GTR35>{{cite news|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/88254/production-mclaren-p1-gtr-revealed-in-geneva|title=Production McLaren P1 GTR revealed in Geneva|first=James | last = Batchelor|work=[[Auto Express]]|date=3 March 2015|access-date=14 March 2015|archive-date=15 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150315020120/http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/88254/production-mclaren-p1-gtr-revealed-in-geneva|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>[https://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/265 McLAREN P1 GTR TO SHOW ITS LIMITED PRODUCTION FORM IN GENEVA]</ref> This price includes a worldwide owners track day series; later cars were offered for less money, for those P1 owners who had no interest in the track series but still wanted to purchase the GTR variant. In total 58 cars were made.<ref name="Top Speed GTR">{{cite web |date=13 June 2014 |title=2016 McLaren P1 GTR |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2016-mclaren-p1-gtr/ |access-date=3 December 2018 |website=Top Speed |archive-date=10 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093315/https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2016-mclaren-p1-gtr/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The P1 GTR went into production in 2015, after all the 375 standard P1s had been built, as a homage to its race-winning ancestor, the [[McLaren F1 GTR]] and were built, maintained and run by McLaren Special Operations.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://media.mclarenautomotive.com/release/257/ |title=McLaren to launch exclusive ultimate track focused McLaren P1 GTR |date=1 July 2014 |website=media.mclarenautomotive.com |access-date=2 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140702060948/http://media.mclarenautomotive.com/release/257/ |archive-date=2 July 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The P1 GTR's hybrid engine is rated at {{convert|1000|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}}, representing an {{convert|84|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} increase over the standard production P1, although McLaren did not disclose whether the power increase was from electrical boost or tuning the twin-turbocharged 3.8-litre V8. Performance figures remain unconfirmed.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/Here-comes-the-986bhp-McLaren-P1-GTR-2014-07-25 |title=Here comes the 986bhp McLaren P1 GTR |publisher=Top Gear |date=25 July 2014 |access-date=30 April 2017 |archive-date=16 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150216005943/http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/Here-comes-the-986bhp-McLaren-P1-GTR-2014-07-25 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The weight of the P1 GTR was reduced by {{cvt|50|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}}, achieving a power-to-weight ratio of {{convert|697|PS|kW hp|abbr=on}} per {{convert|1|tonne}}.<ref>{{cite web|title=Production McLaren P1 GTR revealed with iconic livery|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/88254/production-mclaren-p1-gtr-revealed-with-iconic-livery|publisher=Autoexpress UK|access-date=22 February 2015|archive-date=22 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150222094947/http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/88254/production-mclaren-p1-gtr-revealed-with-iconic-livery|url-status=live}}</ref> This equates to a weight-to-power ratio of {{convert|{{#expr:1440/1000 round 2}}|kg|lb|2|abbr=on}} per horsepower. The car also featured slick tyres, and had greater levels of performance, grip, aerodynamics and downforce in comparison to the road car. Featuring a new fixed ride height on race-prepared suspension, a fixed rear wing capable of using DRS, and a new exclusively designed exhaust made of titanium and [[Inconel]]. Due to its fixed rear wing, the GTR generates 10% more downforce than the road legal P1. The P1 GTR has a kerb weight of {{cvt|1440|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} which includes the weight of the batteries.<ref name="AC">{{cite web|url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/mclaren/p1-gtr-2015-2016|title=McLaren P1 GTR 2015-2016 review|first=Andrew|last=Frankel|publisher=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]|access-date=27 April 2019|archive-date=27 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190427045918/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/mclaren/p1-gtr-2015-2016|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:2016 McLaren P1 GTR LM (Lanzante Motorsport - 6 exemplaires - prix neuf - 3.600.000 €).jpg|thumb|Road legal P1 GTR converted by Lanzante]]
The P1 GTR can accelerate from 0–97&nbsp;km/h (60&nbsp;mph) in under 2.8 seconds, and will go on to reach a limited top speed of {{convert|217|mph|abbr=on|order=flip}}. Additionally, the P1 GTR will brake from {{convert|60|mi/h|km/h|abbr=on|order=flip}} to 0 in {{convert|85|ft|m|abbr=on}}, and can corner at 1.54&nbsp;G long with pulling a lateral acceleration of 2.5&nbsp;g on the skidpad.<ref name="AC"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.evo.co.uk/mclaren/mclaren-p1-gtr|title=McLaren P1 GTR review - we take to the streets in converted road-legal racer|date=8 August 2017|publisher=EVO Magazine|first=Jethro | last = Bovingdon|access-date=20 July 2018|archive-date=20 July 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180720140132/http://www.evo.co.uk/mclaren/mclaren-p1-gtr|url-status=live}}</ref>

In late 2015, historic racing team and [[McLaren F1]] specialists [[Lanzante Motorsport]] started undergoing road conversions of P1 GTRs for owners who wanted to drive their cars on the road. Thus far, 27 P1 GTRs have been converted for road use by Lanzante. The road legal version of the P1 GTR has a claimed top speed of {{cvt|225|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} and a {{cvt|0-60|mph|kph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} time of 2.4 seconds.

To celebrate 40 years since [[James Hunt]] won the Formula 1 Driver’s Championship, McLaren designed a livery for the P1 GTR that was inspired by Hunt’s race helmet, with a black base color combined with red, yellow and blue stripes based on his Wellington College colors. The car was showcased at the 2016 Goodwood Festival of Speed and driven by Bruno Senna, the official McLaren P1 GTR Driver Program Mentor.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-goodwood-festival-of-speed/special-mclaren-p1-gtr-honours-f1-legend-james-hunt|title=Special McLaren P1 GTR honours F1 legend James Hunt at Goodwood|date=10 June 2016|publisher=Autocar UK|first=Matt | last = Burt|access-date=4 November 2023|archive-date=4 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231104102332/https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motor-shows-goodwood-festival-of-speed/special-mclaren-p1-gtr-honours-f1-legend-james-hunt|url-status=live}}</ref>

====P1 GTR-18 (2018)====
In April 2020, Lanzante Motorsport announced that it would do six more road legal conversions of the P1 GTR. This conversion uses the same Longtail bodywork found on the one-off P1 GT as well as a modified rear wing, a larger front splitter, and louvers for an increase in downforce. P1 GTR-18 chassis #1 (GTR chassis #11) is painted in the ‘Gulf Team Davidoff’ no. 028R livery with its wheels finished in no. 028R’s trademark orange shade.<ref>{{Cite web|title=McLaren P1 GTR-18 pays homage to the final F1 GTR Longtail|url=https://www.evo.co.uk/mclaren/mclaren-p1-gtr/202511/mclaren-p1-gtr-18-pays-homage-to-the-final-f1-gtr-longtail|access-date=2021-04-01|website=evo|language=en|archive-date=23 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210423030336/https://www.evo.co.uk/mclaren/mclaren-p1-gtr/202511/mclaren-p1-gtr-18-pays-homage-to-the-final-f1-gtr-longtail|url-status=live}}</ref>
===P1 by MSO (2016)===
It is a limited version of P1 with hand-laid carbon fibre body in Lio Blue tinted lacquer, gloss black wheels, retrim of the interior with carbon black Alcantara and contrasting blue stitching, a 24-carat gold exhaust heatshield.

The vehicle was unveiled in the 2016 Geneva Motor Show.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/341 |title=MSO PAYS CARBON FIBRE TRIBUTE TO McLAREN P1 AND WELCOMES 675LT SPIDER AT 2016 GENEVA MOTOR SHOW |access-date=15 December 2021 |archive-date=15 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211215000731/https://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/341 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://gtspirit.com/2016/02/17/official-full-carbon-mclaren-p1-mso/ |title=Official: Full-Carbon McLaren P1 by MSO |date=17 February 2016 |access-date=15 December 2021 |archive-date=15 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211215000937/https://gtspirit.com/2016/02/17/official-full-carbon-mclaren-p1-mso/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===P1 LM (2016&ndash;2017)===
[[File:FoS20162016 0624 123606AA (27851727806).jpg|thumb|The prototype P1 LM (XP1 LM) at the 2016 [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]]]]
With the production run of the P1 GTR complete, and prompted by their efforts in converting track-only P1 GTRs to road-legal specification, [[Lanzante Motorsport]] commissioned McLaren Special Operations' Bespoke division to build a further total of 6 new P1 GTRs for them to develop into road-legal P1 LM variants.<ref name="Meet McLaren's £3,000,000 Ultra‑Limited P1 LM">{{cite web | url = https://www.goodwood.com/grrc/event-coverage/festival-of-speed/2016/6/lanzante/ | title = Meet McLaren's £3,000,000 Ultra-Limited P1 LM | publisher = Goodwood | date = 24 June 2016 | access-date = 24 June 2016 | archive-date = 5 September 2018 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180905141054/https://www.goodwood.com/grrc/event-coverage/festival-of-speed/2016/6/lanzante/ | url-status = live }}</ref> Of this production run, five P1 LMs were sold and the sixth, the prototype P1 LM codenamed 'XP1 LM', was retained and is now being used for development and testing of future models. In order to convert the cars into the P1 LM specification, Lanzante Motorsport made changes to the drivetrain hardware (to increase power output), employed a modified rear wing and larger front splitter along with dive planes (to improve downforce), removed the air-jack system and installed Inconel catalytic converter pipes and exhaust headers, lightweight fabricated charge coolers, [[Lexan]] windows, lighter seats (similar to those used in the F1 GTR) and a titanium exhaust system, bolts and fixings (to save weight).<ref name="McLaren P1 LM is the world's most extreme, exclusive supercar">{{cite web | url = https://www.autoblog.com/2016/06/22/mclaren-p1-lm-extreme-exclusive-supercar/ | title = McLaren P1 LM is the world's most extreme, exclusive supercar | publisher = [[Autoblog]] | date = 22 June 2016 | access-date = 22 June 2016 | archive-date = 23 June 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160623155934/http://www.autoblog.com/2016/06/22/mclaren-p1-lm-extreme-exclusive-supercar/ | url-status = live }}</ref> The result is a weight reduction of {{cvt|60|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} as compared to the P1 GTR as well as a 40 per cent increase in downforce.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/146750/mclaren-p1-lm-record-nurburgring-lap/|title=Watch The McLaren P1 LM's Record-Setting 6:43 Nurburgring Lap|work=Motor1.com|access-date=5 September 2018|language=en|archive-date=5 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180905141056/https://www.motor1.com/news/146750/mclaren-p1-lm-record-nurburgring-lap/|url-status=live}}</ref> The P1 LM also features a larger twin-turbocharged V8 engine than the P1 and P1 GTR at {{cvt|3994|cc|L|1|abbr=on}} with an 8,500 rpm red line.<ref name=":0" />

The P1 LM has a total power output of {{cvt|1000|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} and {{cvt|1050|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque, with {{cvt|800|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} being delivered at 7,250 rpm and an additional {{cvt|200|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} from its electric motor.<ref name=":0" /> The top speed is limited to {{cvt|345|kph|mph|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name=":0" /> The tyre specifications are 275/30/19 for the front tyres and 335/30/20 for the rear tyres.<ref name=":0" />

At the 2016 [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]], the prototype P1 LM, 'XP1 LM', set the fastest ever time for a road car up the Goodwood hillclimb, with a time of 47.07 seconds, driven by [[Kenny Bräck]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/goodwood-fos-2016/watch-mclarens-p1-lm-set-goodwood-hill-record|title=Watch McLaren's P1 LM set a Goodwood hill record|date=30 June 2016|work=Top Gear|access-date=5 September 2018|language=en|archive-date=5 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180905142509/https://www.topgear.com/car-news/goodwood-fos-2016/watch-mclarens-p1-lm-set-goodwood-hill-record|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 27 April 2017, the prototype P1 LM, XP1 LM, continued its success on track, beating the road car lap record time at the [[Nürburgring]], with a time of 6:43.22 using road legal [[Pirelli]] P Zero Trofeo R tyres but without a front number plate required for a car to be road legal.<ref name=":0" /> This lap time was once again set by [[Kenny Bräck]], and announced on 26 May 2017.<ref name=":0" />

===P1 Spider (2022)===
[[File:2022 McLaren P1 Spider.jpg|thumb|McLaren P1 Spider]]In June 2022, at the Goodwood Festival of Speed, Lanzante unveiled the P1 Spider. The company is offered 5 conversions to the open-top P1 for €2.4m.

===P1 GT (2018)===
[[File:McLaren P1 GT (cropped).jpg|thumb|McLaren P1 GT at the 2018 Goodwood Festival of Speed]]
At the 2018 [[Goodwood Festival of Speed]], [[Lanzante Motorsport]], who had previously modified P1 GTRs to road legal specifications and developed the P1 LM, introduced a new special based on the P1 GTR. The new car, called the P1 GT, was commissioned by two different McLaren VIP customers; one from the United Arab Emirates and one from Japan. Only two were built. The P1 GT is inspired by the [[McLaren F1#F1 GT|McLaren F1 GT]] homologation special from the 1990s, including more aggressive bodywork than the standard car. Exterior modifications include a longer rear section, a larger rear wing, a longer front splitter, vented front fenders, removal of front canards, quad exhaust system in place of the original dual outlet design and a modified rear diffuser. The interior features fixed sports seats and [[Alcantara (material)|Alcantara]] upholstery in tan and green colour along with a racing steering wheel and carbon fibre bits while the exterior features Silverstone green bodywork paying homage to the original homologation special. Powertrain modifications and performance figures remain unknown but are likely to have been increased as compared to the standard car owing to the extensive modifications.
<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a22033778/lanzantes-latest-mclaren-p1-is-a-longtail-gt/|title=Lanzante's Latest McLaren P1 is a Longtail GT|first=Máté|last=Petrány|date=4 July 2018|website=Road & Track|access-date=16 November 2018|archive-date=17 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181117023700/https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a22033778/lanzantes-latest-mclaren-p1-is-a-longtail-gt/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===P1 GTR by McLaren Special Operations (2018)===
Also called 'Beco', it is a bespoke version of P1 GTR commemorating 30th anniversary of [[Ayrton Senna]] securing his first Formula 1 World Championship. It includes a tailwing inspired by the [[McLaren-Honda]] MP4/4 race car, [[Marlboro]] liveries.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/648 |title=Unique McLaren P1 GTR by McLaren Special Operations commemorates 1988 Ayrton Senna maiden championship |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214225728/https://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/648 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2019-mclaren-p1-gtr-by-mclaren-special-operations/ |title=2019 McLaren P1 GTR By McLaren Special Operations |date=20 December 2018 |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=10 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093222/https://www.topspeed.com/cars/mclaren/2019-mclaren-p1-gtr-by-mclaren-special-operations/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Legacy ==
McLaren announced the P1 had completed a sub-seven minute [[list of Nürburgring Nordschleife lap times|lap of the Nürburgring]], which equates to an average speed in excess of {{convert|179|km/h|abbr=on}}, but did not publish the exact time.<ref>{{cite web|title=McLaren confirms P1 Nurburgring lap time|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/84823/mclaren-confirms-p1-nurburgring-lap-time|access-date=7 December 2013|archive-date=9 December 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131209045649/http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/mclaren/p1/84823/mclaren-confirms-p1-nurburgring-lap-time|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.driving.co.uk/news/mclaren/news-mclaren-confirms-sub-7-minute-nurburgring-p1-lap-releases-sensational-video/16778 |title=News: McLaren confirms sub-7-minute Nürburgring P1 lap, releases sensational video &#124; Sunday Times Driving |access-date=8 December 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131212054732/http://www.driving.co.uk/news/mclaren/news-mclaren-confirms-sub-7-minute-nurburgring-p1-lap-releases-sensational-video/16778 |archive-date=12 December 2013 }}</ref> However, the P1 LM, which wasn't road legal during the run, beat the road car's record time at the ''Nordschleife'' with a time of 6:43.22.

==Marketing==
A number of scale models of the P1 have been offered by various companies, including the Mattel [[Hot Wheels]] 1:64 die-cast P1 model car in Volcano Orange and Supernova Silver body, [[Lego]] Speed Champions series Volcano Yellow McLaren P1 model kit,<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.lego.com/en-ca/product/mclaren-p1-75909 |title=McLaren P1 75909 |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=10 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093221/https://www.lego.com/en-ca/product/mclaren-p1-75909?age-gate=grown_up |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Scalextric]] Volcano Yellow and Volcano Orange P1 slot car, [[Maisto]] radio-controlled 1:14 scale McLaren P1, Minichamps and Tecnomodel 1:43 P1 model car, and Amalgam Fine Model Cars 1:8 scale McLaren P1.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20181005134618/http://cars.mclaren.com/featured-articles/masters-in-miniature.html MASTERS IN MINIATURE]</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.amalgamcollection.com/products/mclaren-p1-1-8 |title=McLaren P1 (2012) 1:8 SCALE |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214234623/https://www.amalgamcollection.com/products/mclaren-p1-1-8 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/326 |title=McLARENS IN MINIATURE - STOCKING FILLERS |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=10 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240610093224/https://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/326 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.caranddriver.com/news/a15350585/mclarens-p1-still-in-production-only-slightly-smaller-than-you-remember/ |title=McLaren's P1 Still in Production, Only Slightly Smaller Than You Remember |date=15 December 2015 |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214234632/https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a15350585/mclarens-p1-still-in-production-only-slightly-smaller-than-you-remember/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

As part of McLaren Automotive's development of a pure electric vehicle for its Ultimate Series, a miniature version of electric ride on McLaren P1 in Volcano Yellow body colour went on sale at the end of October 2016 at selected McLaren Automotive retailers, followed by recognised global toy retailers.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/401 |title=THE LATEST McLAREN P1 IS PURE ELECTRIC |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=24 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200924235846/https://cars.mclaren.press/ww/releases/401 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.ctvnews.ca/business/autos/article/ride-on-mclaren-unveils-a-new-p1-its-first-all-electric-car/ |title=Ride On: McLaren unveils a new P1, its first all-electric car |date=26 September 2016 |access-date=14 December 2021 |archive-date=14 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211214230512/https://www.ctvnews.ca/autos/ride-on-mclaren-unveils-a-new-p1-its-first-all-electric-car-1.3089036 |url-status=live }}</ref>

